# 1️⃣ Find an Element in an Array

### 🎯 Problem

Given an array and a target element, check whether the target exists.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class FindEleInArray {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 25, 7, 40, 15};
        int target = 40;

        boolean found = false;

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if(arr[i] == target) {
                found = true;
            }
        }

        if(found) {
            System.out.println("Element Found");
        }
        else {
            System.out.println("Element Not Found");
        }
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
Element Found
```

### 🧠 Logic

Initially:

```java
boolean found = false;
```

We assume the element is not present.

When we find it:

```java
if(arr[i] == target) {
    found = true;
}
```

Then `found` becomes `true`.

### 🔍 Dry Run

```text
Array = {10, 25, 7, 40, 15}
Target = 40

10 == 40 → false
25 == 40 → false
7  == 40 → false
40 == 40 → true
15 == 40 → false
```

Final:

```text
found = true
```

### ⭐ Interview Tip

This is called **Linear Search**.

The array is checked one element at a time.

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 2️⃣ Find the Index of an Element

### 🎯 Problem

Find the index of the target element.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class IndexOfElement {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 25, 7, 40, 15};
        int target = 7;

        int index = -1;

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if(arr[i] == target) {
                index = i;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Element Found At Index = " + index);
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
Element Found At Index = 2
```

### 🧠 Important Concept

```java
int index = -1;
```

`-1` means:

> Element has not been found.

Why `-1`?

Because valid array indexes start from `0`.

```text
0 → valid
1 → valid
2 → valid
...
-1 → not found
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

```text
Index:   0   1   2   3   4
Value:  10  25   7  40  15

Target = 7

i = 0 → 10 ≠ 7
i = 1 → 25 ≠ 7
i = 2 → 7 == 7

index = 2
```

### 🧠 Important

```text
i       → index
arr[i]  → value
```

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 3️⃣ Count Occurrences of an Element

### 🎯 Problem

Count how many times the target appears.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class OccurenceOfAnElement {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {2, 5, 2, 7, 2, 9};
        int target = 2;

        int count = 0;

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if(arr[i] == target) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Occurences = " + count);
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
Occurences = 3
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

```text
Array = {2, 5, 2, 7, 2, 9}
Target = 2

2 → count = 1
5 → no
2 → count = 2
7 → no
2 → count = 3
9 → no
```

Final:

```text
count = 3
```

### 🧠 Memory Trick

> **If I find what I'm looking for → count it.**

Basic pattern:

```java
int count = 0;

if(condition) {
    count++;
}
```

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 4️⃣ Find the Largest Element

### 🎯 Problem

Find the largest element in an array.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class LargestEle {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 25, 7, 40, 15};

        int largest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if(arr[i] > largest) {
                largest = arr[i];
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Largest = " + largest);
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
Largest = 40
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

```text
largest = -∞

10 > -∞ → largest = 10
25 > 10 → largest = 25
7  > 25 → no
40 > 25 → largest = 40
15 > 40 → no
```

Final:

```text
largest = 40
```

### 🧠 Important

Using:

```java
Integer.MIN_VALUE
```

is safer than using `0`.

For example:

```java
int[] arr = {-10, -25, -7};
```

The largest value is `-7`.

### 🧠 Memory Trick

> **Largest wants `>`**

```java
if(arr[i] > largest)
```

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 5️⃣ Find the Smallest Element

### 🎯 Problem

Find the smallest element.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class SmallestEleInArray {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 25, 7, 40, 15};

        int smallest = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if(arr[i] < smallest) {
                smallest = arr[i];
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Smallest = " + smallest);
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
Smallest = 7
```

### 🧠 Important

Use:

```java
Integer.MAX_VALUE
```

as the starting value.

### 🧠 Memory Trick

> **Smallest wants `<`**

```java
if(arr[i] < smallest)
```

### 🔄 Remember the Pair

```text
Largest:
MIN_VALUE + >

Smallest:
MAX_VALUE + <
```

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 6️⃣ Find the Second Largest Element

### 🎯 Problem

Find the second largest element without sorting.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class SecondLargestEle {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 25, 7, 40, 15};

        int largest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        int secondLargest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if(arr[i] > largest) {

                secondLargest = largest;
                largest = arr[i];
            }
        }

        System.out.println(
            "Second Largest = " + secondLargest
        );
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
Second Largest = 25
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

```text
10 → L = 10, S = -∞

25 → L = 25, S = 10

7  → no change

40 → L = 40, S = 25

15 → no change
```

Final:

```text
largest = 40
secondLargest = 25
```

### 🧠 Important Pattern

When a new largest appears:

```java
secondLargest = largest;
largest = arr[i];
```

Think:

> **New champion arrives → old champion becomes second.**

### ⚠️ Note

This basic version assumes the problem is asking for the second-largest position/value in the usual distinct-value setting. Handling duplicates requires an additional condition.

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 7️⃣ Reverse an Array

### 🎯 Problem

Print an array in reverse order.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class ReverseAnArray {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        for(int i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {

            System.out.print(arr[i] + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
50 40 30 20 10
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

```text
Index:  0   1   2   3   4
Value: 10  20  30  40  50

i = 4 → 50
i = 3 → 40
i = 2 → 30
i = 1 → 20
i = 0 → 10
```

### 🧠 Important Concept

For reverse traversal:

```java
for(int i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
```

### ⚠️ Important Difference

For sum:

```java
sum = sum + arr[i];
```

For reverse printing:

```java
System.out.print(arr[i]);
```

### 🧠 Memory Trick

> **Reverse = start from the last index and move backward.**

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 8️⃣ Sum of Elements at Even Indexes

### 🎯 Problem

Find the sum of values stored at even indexes.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class SumOfEleAtEvenIndexes {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60};

        int sum = 0;

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if(i % 2 == 0) {
                sum = sum + arr[i];
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Sum = " + sum);
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
Sum = 90
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

```text
Index:   0    1    2    3    4    5
Value:  10   20   30   40   50   60

Even indexes:
0 → 10
2 → 30
4 → 50

10 + 30 + 50 = 90
```

### 🧠 Very Important

```text
i       → index
arr[i]  → value
```

So:

```java
i % 2 == 0
```

checks the **index**.

And:

```java
arr[i]
```

gets the **value**.

### 🧠 Memory Trick

> **`i` = where 📍**  
> **`arr[i]` = what 📦**

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 9️⃣ Largest Element at an Even Index

### 🎯 Problem

Find the largest value among elements located at even indexes.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class LargestAtEvenIndex {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 25, 30, 15, 50, 5};

        int largest = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if(i % 2 == 0 && arr[i] > largest) {

                largest = arr[i];
            }
        }

        System.out.println(
            "Largest at even index = " + largest
        );
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
Largest at even index = 50
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

Only even indexes:

```text
i = 0 → 10
i = 2 → 30
i = 4 → 50
```

Largest:

```text
50
```

### 🧠 Important Pattern

We combine two conditions:

```java
i % 2 == 0
```

→ even index

and:

```java
arr[i] > largest
```

→ larger value

Together:

```java
if(i % 2 == 0 && arr[i] > largest)
```

### 🧠 Interview Pattern

> **Filter first → then calculate.**

Here:

```text
Filter → even indexes
Calculate → maximum
```

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 🔟 Smallest Element at an Odd Index

### 🎯 Problem

Find the smallest value among elements at odd indexes.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p3;

public class SmallestEleAtOddIndex {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 25, 30, 15, 50, 5};

        int smallest = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

        for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if(i % 2 != 0 && arr[i] < smallest) {

                smallest = arr[i];
            }
        }

        System.out.println(
            "Smallest at odd index = " + smallest
        );
    }
}
```

### 📤 Output

```text
Smallest at odd index = 5
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

Odd indexes:

```text
Index:   1    3    5
Value:  25   15    5
```

Smallest:

```text
5
```

### 🧠 Important Pattern

Odd index:

```java
i % 2 != 0
```

Smallest:

```java
arr[i] < smallest
```

Combined:

```java
if(i % 2 != 0 && arr[i] < smallest)
```

### ⏱ Complexity

- Time: `O(n)`
- Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 🧠 Day 5 DSA — Master Patterns

## 1. Array Traversal

The most important basic pattern:

```java
for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

    // use arr[i]
}
```

---

## 2. Index vs Value

This is extremely important.

```text
i       → index / position
arr[i]  → value
```

Example:

```text
Index:   0    1    2
Value:  10   20   30
```

If:

```java
i = 2;
```

then:

```java
arr[i] = 30;
```

### 🧠 Memory Trick

> **`i` = WHERE**  
> **`arr[i]` = WHAT**

---

